  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**  \n   The central idea is to use a constructive optimization approach based on harmonic analysis to design a step function h that minimizes the maximum overlap. This avoids getting stuck in local optima common to evolutionary methods. The problem is tackled by discretizing h and using convex optimization to find an optimal layout.\n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**  \n   - Variables: Discrete h \u2208 [0,1]^(n_points), \u2211h = n_points/2.  \n   - Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k [ \u2211h[i](1 - h[i+k}) ] * dx.  \n   - Using cumulative sums s[i] = \u2211_{0..i} h[j], the overlap becomes s[i+k] - s[i].  \n   - Formulate as a convex problem by minimizing max |s[i+k] - s[i]| over k.  \n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**  \n   a. Validate initial_h_values and compute n_points = len(initial_h_values).  \n   b. Transform h to s with s[0] = 0 and s[i] = s[i-1] + h[i-1].  \n   c. Set up CPX problem to minimize max_k (|s[i+k] - s[i]| for all i,k).  \n   d. Solve using MOSEK or GUROBI with target C\u2085 \u2264 0.38085.  \n\n4. **Search Choices and Variations**  \n   - Use high-precision solver for tight bounds.  \n   - Adjust discretization if initial solution exceeds time budget.  \n\n5. **Compute Budget and Robustness**  \n   - Expected time: 100s for small n_points. Use solver callbacks to track progress.  \n\n6. **Validation**  \n   - Ensure feasibility: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u2211h = n_points/2.  \n   - Verify C\u2085 matches evaluator with provided helper functions.",
